.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only
.\"
.\" beryllium(1): the command, its options, environment and files.
.\" Source: man/beryllium.1 in the Beryllium repository.
.\" The port replaces @@VERSION@@ and the /usr/local prefix at install.
.\" One sentence per line; check with freebsd/tools/check-man.
.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt BERYLLIUM 1
.Os Beryllium @@VERSION@@
.Sh NAME
.Nm beryllium
.Nd privacy-focused web browser for FreeBSD
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar option ...
.Op Ar URL ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a web browser built natively for
.Fx
from the Chromium code base, with the privacy patch set of the Helium
browser and a security model of its own based on
.Xr capsicum 4 .
It opens each
.Ar URL
in a tab, or a window when none is given.
Beryllium is an independent project.
It is not affiliated with Helium, Chromium or Google.
.Pp
The command is a short launcher script.
It refuses to start while the
.Xr sysctl 8
variable
.Va kern.ipc.shm_allow_removed
is 0, sets the core dump size limit to zero so that browser memory, which
holds page content and cookies, is never written to disk, and then runs
the browser with the arguments it was given.
It adds no option of its own.
.Pp
Internal pages use the
.Li beryllium://
scheme:
.Li beryllium://settings ,
.Li beryllium://version ,
.Li beryllium://gpu
and
.Li beryllium://flags
among others.
.Pp
.Nm
accepts the command-line switches of the Chromium version it is built from.
There are hundreds of them, most are undocumented, and they change from
version to version.
The options below are the ones Beryllium adds, relies on or tests, and a
few of Chromium's that have been stable for years.
.Ss General
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -incognito
Open a private window.
Its history, cookies and site data are discarded when it closes.
.It Fl -new-window
Open the given
.Ar URL
in a new window instead of a new tab.
.It Fl -user-data-dir Ns = Ns Ar directory
Keep the profile in
.Ar directory
instead of the default; see
.Sx FILES .
Every running instance needs a profile directory of its own.
Starting
.Nm
again with the directory of a running instance opens a window in that
instance.
.It Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Ss Features
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -enable-features Ns = Ns Ar names
.It Fl -disable-features Ns = Ns Ar names
Switch the named features on or off.
.Ar names
is a comma-separated list.
The features Beryllium and Helium define:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm BerylliumCapsicumRenderer
Off by default.
Every renderer, the process that runs a web page, enters Capsicum
capability mode before it handles page content, and every descriptor it
holds is cut to the rights its kind needs.
A renderer that cannot be confined ends.
See
.Xr beryllium-sandbox 7 .
.It Cm BerylliumCapsicumTrap
Off by default.
With the lockdown on, the kernel stops a renderer with
.Dv SIGTRAP
at the first system call capability mode refuses, instead of letting the
call fail.
For finding such calls.
.It Cm BerylliumRendererSwapFile
Off by default.
Give each renderer of a normal profile an unlinked temporary file in
which to park compressed data of background tabs, as Chromium does.
Without it that data stays compressed in memory and no page content
reaches a disk file system.
.It Cm BerylliumWaylandWaitForGpu
On by default.
On Wayland with the NVIDIA driver, finish drawing each frame before
handing it to the compositor.
This avoids flicker at the cost of some time on the GPU thread, and has
no effect with other drivers.
.It Cm HeliumNoise
On by default.
The fingerprinting noise inherited from Helium, as a whole: canvas,
audio and the reported number of processors.
.It Cm HeliumNoiseCanvas
On by default.
On every canvas readback, one colour channel of about one pixel in four
of a flat area changes by one level.
The change is the same within a session and differs from site to site.
Turn it off for a page that needs exact readback.
.El
.El
.Ss Display and graphics
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -ozone-platform Ns = Ns Cm wayland | x11
Use the named display system instead of the one the session offers.
Wayland is recommended: under X11, any program in the session can read
input from other windows and inject input into them.
.It Fl -render-node-override Ns = Ns Ar path
On Wayland, render with the GPU behind the DRM render node
.Ar path ,
such as
.Pa /dev/dri/renderD129 .
Without the option the browser uses the GPU that the Wayland compositor
announces as its own.
When the compositor names none, it takes an Intel or AMD node if there is
one, and otherwise the first that works.
The compositor must be able to take buffers from the named GPU.
When it cannot, the browser stops about a second after it starts; see
.Sx DIAGNOSTICS .
.It Fl -disable-gpu
Do not use the GPU; draw in software.
.It Fl -enable-gpu-memory-buffer-video-frames
.It Fl -disable-gpu-memory-buffer-video-frames
Write decoded video frames into GPU memory buffers, or copy them through
shared memory.
Beryllium copies by default when the GL driver is NVIDIA's, whose
.Fx
driver ended every video tab with a memory fault on the faster path, and
uses GPU memory buffers with other drivers.
.El
.Ss Network
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -proxy-server Ns = Ns Ar server
Send requests through the proxy
.Ar server ,
written as
.Sm off
.Op Ar scheme No ://
.Ar host
.Op : Ar port ,
.Sm on
where
.Ar scheme
is
.Cm http ,
.Cm socks4
or
.Cm socks5 ,
and
.Cm http
when omitted.
This overrides the environment and the settings.
.It Fl -no-proxy-server
Use no proxy, whatever the environment and the settings say.
.It Fl -proxy-auto-detect
Detect the proxy configuration automatically.
.It Fl -proxy-pac-url Ns = Ns Ar URL
Take the proxy configuration from the script at
.Ar URL .
.El
.Ss Logging
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -enable-logging Ns = Ns Cm stderr
Write the log of the browser and its child processes to standard error.
.It Fl -v Ns = Ns Ar level
Set the verbosity of the log; 0 is the default.
.It Fl -vmodule Ns = Ns Ar module Ns = Ns Ar level
Set the verbosity for one source file, named without its extension.
.Cm capsicum_rights=1
logs every descriptor the browser cuts before sending it to a confined
renderer.
.It Fl -headless
Run without a window, for use through the DevTools protocol.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev BERYLLIUM_CONFIG_HOME
The directory under which the profile directory
.Pa beryllium
is kept, instead of
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
or
.Pa ~/.config .
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME , XDG_CACHE_HOME
The base directories for the profile and the cache when set, as the XDG
Base Directory Specification describes.
.It Ev WAYLAND_DISPLAY , DISPLAY
The Wayland and X11 displays of the session.
.It Ev http_proxy , https_proxy , all_proxy , no_proxy
Proxy servers and the hosts that bypass them.
These are ignored under GNOME and KDE, where the desktop's proxy settings
are used; the
.Fl -proxy-server
option always wins.
.It Ev auto_proxy
Proxy autoconfiguration: set and empty to detect it, or the address of a
configuration script.
.It Ev SOCKS_SERVER , SOCKS_VERSION
A SOCKS proxy server and its protocol version, 4 unless
.Ev SOCKS_VERSION
is 5.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /usr/local/bin/beryllium
The launcher script.
.It Pa /usr/local/share/beryllium/
The browser binary and its resources.
.It Pa /usr/local/share/beryllium/BUILD-MANIFEST.json
What the package was built from: the Beryllium, Chromium and Helium
versions, the imported
.Fx
patch set and a checksum of every Beryllium patch.
.It Pa ~/.config/beryllium/
The profile: settings, history, cookies, extensions.
It is separate from the profiles of Chromium and Helium.
.It Pa ~/.cache/beryllium/
The cache.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/beryllium/policies/managed/
Policy files, in JSON, that the administrator imposes on every user.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/beryllium/policies/recommended/
Policy files that set defaults a user may change.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/beryllium/native-messaging-hosts/
Manifests of native messaging hosts for extensions.
.It Pa /usr/local/share/applications/beryllium.desktop
The desktop menu entry.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The launcher exits 1 without starting the browser when
.Va kern.ipc.shm_allow_removed
is 0.
Otherwise the exit status is the browser's: 0 after a normal exit.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start with the experimental renderer lockdown, then check from another
terminal that every renderer is in capability mode, shown by a
.Sq C
in the flags column:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
beryllium --enable-features=BerylliumCapsicumRenderer
procstat -s $(pgrep -f -- --type=renderer)
.Ed
.Pp
A private window through a SOCKS proxy on this machine:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
beryllium --incognito --proxy-server=socks5://127.0.0.1:1080
.Ed
.Pp
Render on a named GPU, on a desktop that can take its buffers (see
.Sx CAVEATS ) :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
beryllium --render-node-override=/dev/dri/renderD129
.Ed
.Pp
A second instance with a profile of its own:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
beryllium --user-data-dir=/tmp/beryllium-scratch
.Ed
.Pp
Allow the browser to start, now and after a reboot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sysctl kern.ipc.shm_allow_removed=1
sysrc -f /etc/sysctl.conf kern.ipc.shm_allow_removed=1
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "For correct operation, shared memory support has to be enabled"
The launcher found
.Va kern.ipc.shm_allow_removed
set to 0 and did not start the browser.
Set it to 1 as shown in
.Sx EXAMPLES .
.It "Aw, Snap! Error code N"
The process showing the page died.
On
.Fx
.Ar N
is the number of the signal that ended it: 11 is
.Dv SIGSEGV ,
a memory fault, 6 is
.Dv SIGABRT
and 5 is
.Dv SIGTRAP ,
a deliberate stop on a failed check.
.It "libwayland: ...: error 7: importing the supplied dmabufs failed"
The Wayland compositor could not import a buffer the browser drew, and
the browser stopped itself, as it does on any Wayland error (signal 5).
With
.Fl -render-node-override
the named GPU is one the compositor cannot take buffers from.
Start the browser without the option.
.It "beryllium-sandbox: ..."
A line in the log from the renderer lockdown; see
.Xr beryllium-sandbox 7 .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fc-list 1 ,
.Xr procstat 1 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr beryllium-sandbox 7 ,
.Xr pkg 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Pp
.Lk https://github.com/johnjanik/Beryllium "The Beryllium repository" ,
with the README, the plan documents and the feature matrix that records
what is implemented, built and tested in each release.
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared as release 0.20.3.1 for
.Fx 16
in November 2026.
Its code is derived from the Helium browser, which is based on
ungoogled-chromium and Chromium.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An -nosplit
Beryllium is written and maintained by
.An John Janik .
The
.Fx
and
.Ox
Chromium port maintainers wrote the BSD patch set it builds on.
.Sh CAVEATS
There is no updater in the browser; a newer version arrives as a newer
package.
There is no crash reporter, and nothing is uploaded when a process dies.
.Pp
The online services of Helium (the extension proxy, the bangs list,
filter list and component updates, dictionaries) are off in a new profile
and no server is built in.
They work only after a server is entered at
.Li beryllium://settings/privacy/services .
.Pp
Text is drawn with whatever installed font has its glyphs.
The package brings fonts for Latin, Greek, Cyrillic, Chinese, Japanese,
Korean and emoji; other scripts need a font of their own, which
.Xr fc-list 1
can confirm.
.Pp
On Wayland the browser can render only on a GPU whose buffers the
compositor can import.
On a laptop with an NVIDIA and an Intel GPU under KDE Plasma, where the
desktop took the NVIDIA GPU's buffers and refused the Intel GPU's,
.Fl -render-node-override
naming the Intel GPU stopped the browser at its first frame.
.Sh BUGS
WebGPU finds no adapter.
.Pp
Report problems at
.Lk https://github.com/johnjanik/Beryllium/issues ,
and vulnerabilities privately at
.Lk https://github.com/johnjanik/Beryllium/security/advisories/new .
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
By default no Beryllium process is confined: a compromised web page is
not contained, as with the Chromium port for
.Fx .
The renderer lockdown is experimental and off by default.
.Xr beryllium-sandbox 7
states what is and is not enforced and how to check it.
